AB  - With the widespread adoption of cloud computing and microservice architectures, software system complexity has grown exponentially, making traditional operation and maintenance (O&M) paradigms relying on predefined rules and manual expertise unsustainable. Artificial Intelligence for IT Operations (AIOps) has thus emerged as a promising solution [1]. This review focuses on mainstream AIOps practice for large-scale cloud-native software systems from 2020 to 2025. Unlike prior surveys centered on either multimodal data fusion or LLM-based AIOps, it integrates multimodal observability data, anomaly detection and root cause analysis methods, and recent OpsLLM applications into a unified framework. This paper systematically sorts out core AIOps technologies, including multimodal data analysis based on logs, metrics, and traces, the evolution of anomaly detection from statistical methods to deep learning, and root cause localization algorithms based on topological graphs and causal inference. It also summarizes typical application scenarios of operation-specific LLMs (OpsLLM) from 2023 to 2025. Finally, it identifies core challenges of current AIOps, such as data annotation bottlenecks and insufficient model interpretability, and outlooks future development trends.
ER  - 

