AB  - Modern cloud platforms and large distributed systems generate massive volumes of logs that record failures, access behavior, and system states. These logs are central to security monitoring and reliability engineering. While recent log analysis models can detect anomalies with high accuracy, operational triage remains difficult. Security teams still need to manually inspect long log sequences, and emerging LLM-based assistants often produce explanations that lack sufficient evidence. More critically, cloud logs are untrusted inputs, and malicious strings embedded in logs can manipulate automated assistants and trigger unsafe outputs. This paper presents EB-AIOps, an evidence-bound and injection-resilient framework for intelligent cloud security triage. The proposed system integrates structured log parsing, risk-aware incident detection, evidence retrieval, and controlled language-model-based reporting. Only high-risk windows are forwarded for triage. For each suspected incident, the framework retrieves a small set of relevant historical logs and constrains the assistant to generate reports strictly grounded in this evidence. Raw log text is isolated from system instructions, which limits the effect of prompt injection hidden inside logs. Experiments on large-scale distributed log datasets show that EB-AIOps achieves strong detection performance, reaching 0.991 accuracy, 0.984 F1-score, and 0.993 AUC, outperforming deep sequence and transformer-based baselines. The framework also improves cross-system generalization, with F1-scores above 0.93 under domain shift. More importantly, EB-AIOps produces highly grounded triage outputs with 0.97 evidence precision and reduces prompt-injection attack success to 0.04, compared to 0.42 for unconstrained assistants. These results demonstrate that EB-AIOps enables accurate, verifiable, and secure incident triage for real cloud security operations.
ER  - 

