AB  - Cloud infrastructure failures cost enterprises over $14,000 per minute of unplanned downtime, and a single faulty update crashed 8.5 million systems worldwide in July 2024. AIOps, the application of artificial intelligence to IT operations, promises automated detection, diagnosis, and remediation. Production adoption, though, has stalled at reactive levels despite two decades of autonomic computing research. This position paper presents a practitioner critique of current AIOps research, drawing from 12 years of production cloud operations across telecommunications, e-commerce, and SaaS infrastructure. A complementary systematic review of 105 self-healing cloud computing papers grounds the critique in published evidence. We identify five disconnects between academic proposals and production realities: evaluation on synthetic workloads that miss cascading failure dynamics, an assumption of clean telemetry that does not hold in production, neglect of operator trust and explainability requirements, absence of incremental adoption paths for brownfield environments, and failure to integrate with the observability tools that most organizations already run in production. We propose a five-level AIOps maturity model mapping production adoption stages to research readiness, and we outline a practitioner-informed research agenda targeting each disconnect. Bridging these disconnects is essential for deploying self-healing systems that strengthen infrastructure resilience at national scale.
ER  - 

