AB  - Modern AIOps systems employ machine learning models for anomaly detection, and these models are at risk of gradual performance degradation due to the evolutionary nature of these large-scale distributed systems which often begins to manifest as subtle changes in the execution behavior of the system being monitored. Conventional drift detection techniques in AIOps primarily rely on metric-based drift detection or model-output based drift signals which frequently fail to capture these subtle behavioral changes before they develop into outages of massive proportions. This paper proposes a semantic validation framework for early drift detection which leverages large language models to reason over structured distributed system traces which maintain the contextual relationships between dependencies. The proposed hybrid architecture combines lightweight drift alerts as initial triggers with hierarchical LLM-based semantic validation to balance scalability and accuracy. Drift is identified through multi-level summarization of spans and traces captured in system traces, temporal aggregation of summaries and intent-behavior alignment against established healthy baselines. Experimental evaluation in a simulated microservice environment with controlled drift injections demonstrates significant improvement in detection precision and reduction in mean time to detect when compared to traditional metric-based approaches. Sensitivity analysis across models and aggregation depths further validates the robustness of the framework and highlight the value of semantic reasoning over traces for proactive drift detection in AIOps for complex distributed systems.
ER  - 

