AB  - Accurate forecasting of CPU utilization in largescale IT operations is critical for proactive resource scheduling, anomaly mitigation, and cost-efficient cloud management. However, existing approaches exhibit three notable limitations: (1) statistical models such as ARIMA and vanilla Holt-Winters (HW) fail to capture the nonlinear, spike-prone dynamics embedded in the residual component of periodic workload signals; (2) purely data-driven recurrent models (LSTM, GRU) do not explicitly exploit the strong $\mathbf{2 4}$-hour seasonality prevalent in AIOps metrics, leading to degraded long-horizon accuracy; (3) hybrid decom-position-learning pipelines in prior work rely on vanilla GRU decoders that lack sufficient temporal receptive fields to model multi-scale residual patterns. To address these limitations, we propose HW-TCN-GRU, a two-stage framework that (1) applies multiplicative Holt-Winters decomposition to isolate a structured residual sequence from the raw CPU signal; (2) encodes the residual with a dilated Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) whose exponentially growing receptive field captures multi-scale dependencies; and (3) decodes the TCN representation through a GRU module whose gating mechanism models short-range nonlinear dynamics before additively fusing the result with the HW forecast. Extensive experiments on AIOps benchmark CPU time series demonstrate that HW-TCN-GRU achieves an MAE of 1.31 and RMSE of 1.60, outperforming the best baseline by up to 48.9 % on MAE, while maintaining robust generalization across multiple KPI streams and forecast horizons.
ER  - 

