AB  - The operations and maintenance (O&M) of 5G/6G networks face unprecedented complexity due to virtualization and heterogeneity, with traditional manual approaches resulting in Mean Time To Repair (MTTR) of $\mathbf{6 0 - 1 2 0}$ minutes. While Large Language Models (LLMs) enable new AIOps capabilities, existing systems suffer from three critical limitations: lack of closed-loop execution, inadequate human-machine collaboration, and weak permission control. This paper proposes GO-MAF (GraphOrchestrated Multi-Agent Framework), a human-machine collaborative system for $5 \mathrm{G} / 6 \mathrm{G}$ self-healing operations. Through graph workflow orchestration, domain-specific agents, context management, and a TBAC-RBAC fusion permission model, GOMAF achieves interruptible closed-loop processes with dynamic least-privilege control. Live network experiments with 32,487 real alarms achieved 89.3% closed-loop success rate, 66.0% MTTR reduction (to 24.6 minutes), zero permission violations, and 23.1 % human intervention rate. This research provides a technically feasible solution balancing efficiency and security for $\mathbf{5 G} \boldsymbol{/} \mathbf{6 G}$ intelligent operations, advancing AIOps from “intelligent assistance” to “secure autonomy”.
ER  - 

