AB  - Manual monitoring cannot keep pace with autonomous AI services whose logs contain dense temporal signals and cross-component dependencies. This paper proposes a log-driven AIOps framework that uses graph-based anomaly detection, Bayesian risk ranking, and feedback-guided online learning to monitor AI systems. Runtime logs are converted into temporal event graphs, relationship-sensitive graph scoring captures cascading failures, and probabilistic reasoning suppresses alarms that lack operational severity. A lightweight feedback loop further updates model parameters from ticket closure and operator review. Production-log experiments show that the framework reaches 92.9% F1 and 95.6% key-event coverage, while reducing the false positive rate to 7.1%. The results indicate that structural dependency modeling and posterior risk calibration can improve automated operations without exceeding online latency constraints.
