AB  - Modern applications, especially those built on microservices, produce large volumes of observability data, with logs accounting for a significant portion of this data, making effective monitoring essential. The widespread deployment of cloud applications has led operations teams to collect excessive data out of “fear of missing out,” resulting in significant costs for storage and analysis. This paper demonstrates a dynamic logging framework proposed in our previous research [1], focusing on when, where, and at what granularity to collect log data. The system selectively captures logs from unhealthy microservices and their neighbors, reducing data collection by 95% while maintaining log quality for downstream downstream AI for IT operations (AIOps) tasks [2], thus facilitating effective fault identification and mitigation.
