AB  - As digital transformation accelerates, large-scale campus networks face escalating challenges characterized by heterogeneous environments, massive user bases, and laborintensive operations. Traditional Operations and Maintenance (O&M) models, reliant on manual intervention, struggle with slow response times and human errors. While Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a promising path toward intelligent automation, their direct application in production environments is hindered by hallucinations and the inability to maintain accurate state over long operational sequences. To address these issues, this paper proposes and implements a Multi-Agent AIOps framework. The innovation lies in a cognitive orchestration layer that integrates Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), bolstered by an external State Manager. This manager decouples execution status from the LLM's context window, effectively solving memory deficiencies in continuous tasks. We evaluate the framework using a real-world dataset, AICOC-Eval-105, collected from a production environment. Experiments demonstrate that while standalone LLMs falter in multi-turn operations, our framework—equipped with the State Manager–eliminates hallucinations and achieves high reliability, providing a validated paradigm for the AI-driven Campus Operation Center.
