AB  - The primary goal of AIOps in network security is to detect abnormalities within the firewall ecosystem before they impact business continuity. However, monitoring systems typically utilize thousands of granular detectors, creating operational bottlenecks known as Alert Fatigue, where critical root causes are buried under symptomatic noise. Traditional correlation engines relying on rigid rules fail to aggregate these disjointed alerts into cohesive narratives. We propose a novel Agentic Correlation Engine that transitions from manual logic to dynamic, graph-based agentic reasoning. We introduce a methodology for autonomously constructing high-fidelity Logical Dependency Graphs from unstructured technical documentation using Large Language Models (LLMs). The system employs a dual-strategy clustering mechanism-grouping alerts Vertically via causal chains and Horizontally via peer similarity. Furthermore, a hybrid confidence scoring algorithm validates theoretical graph relationships against real-world alert sequencing using statistical cross-correlation and topological dominance. This architecture effectively operationalizes institutional knowledge, significantly reducing Mean Time To Resolution (MTTR).
ER  - 

