AB  - Rapid advances in artificial intelligence and cloud computing are reshaping higher-education practice, yet adoption remains uneven in institutions with differing levels of digital maturity. This case study evaluates a remote, cross-border knowledge-transfer initiative designed to build capacity for responsible and informed engagement with emerging AI technologies. A UK university delivered a webinar masterclass on Generative AI and AI-cloud integration to students and staff at an Indian institution, using accessible, low-barrier digital platforms. A mixed methods pre/post design examined changes in familiarity, confidence, perceived risks and intended practice. Although 83% of participants reported prior exposure to Generative AI, only 9% felt highly confident using it, and concerns about critical-thinking erosion (70%) and academic-integrity risks (39%) were prominent. Post-session, 90% reported increased confidence in responsible use and 95% intended to adapt their academic or professional practice. Qualitative insights gathered through the expert discussion panel, live Q&A and interactive polls indicated a richer understanding of how AI systems depend on cloud infrastructures for scalability, deployment and real-time processing. Participants described gaining clearer insight into concepts such as distributed training, cloud-based inference pipelines and platform services, which helped them recognise the skill requirements associated with emerging career pathways including ML/AIOps, cloud engineering, cloud architect, and AI governance. The study demonstrates how a scalable, low-barrier webinar model can support cross-border technology knowledge transfer and strengthen capacity for responsible AI adoption in diverse educational contexts.
ER  - 

